%HISTORY

Airport demonstrates native Motif drag and drop with two airports in one window.
Each airport starts with four planes and has eight gates. The number of gates
never grows with the window width. Gate contents and flight numbers survive
resizing; Reset gates explicitly restores the initial stock.

Fly a plane
-----------
Drag an occupied gate's plane with the LEFT mouse button to the other airport
and release anywhere in its gates/runway area. Middle-button dragging remains
supported. Escape cancels the drag. An unsuccessful drop returns the plane to
its original gate. Incoming traffic highlights the destination runway.

For click or keyboard operation, select an occupied gate and choose
"Fly selected plane", or press Enter. Arrow keys move the gate selection.
The outgoing/incoming labels are indicators, not inert radio-button controls.
The flight log announces selection, departures, arrivals, rejected drops and
cancellations. How to fly opens these instructions in the application.

A full airport rejects arrivals without removing a plane from its source.
Separate running instances also exchange planes via the FLIGHT selection,
using format 32 and one flight number. The receiving transfer reserves a gate;
its destroy callback releases a reservation if the transfer does not finish.
Only successful completion removes the source plane. Drop-help/copy operations
are rejected safely. No global X-server keyboard/bell settings are modified.

Appearance and window size
--------------------------
Gallery-launched instances inherit its current theme, density and effective
scale. Direct launches accept standard Xt resources, for example:

  airport -xrm 'ckCoreMotif.appearanceProfile: modern-dark' -xrm 'Xft.dpi: 144'

The example selects modern dark at 150 percent. Classic remains available.
Planes are scale-aware transparent silhouettes. Custom trackBackground,
trackForeground, spotForeground, dragUnderBackground/dragUnderForeground and
flying/valid/invalid/none cursor colors remain application resources. Defaults
come from the current toolkit palette. XmdAirport.ad contains optional examples.
The content scrolls on small screens and at large scales, preserving usable
controls rather than compressing gates and labels into each other.

Dynamic drag-and-drop is the default. The standard
*dragInitiatorProtocolStyle / *dragReceiverProtocolStyle resources can select
other supported Motif protocols, including preregister.

Validation
----------
Run tests/automated/run-airport-behavior.py with an installed --prefix and a fresh
--out directory; pass --gallery to qualify gallery launch inheritance too.
It uses a separate Xvfb server and real XTest input, never the user's desktop.
--state-report FILE (or XM_AIRPORT_STATE_REPORT) optionally emits diagnostic
JSON for this fixture. It has no effect on ordinary interactive launches.
